<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William of Gellone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 755–812</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint William of Gellone, William of Toulouse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William of Gellone was a Frankish noble and kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> who became count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> around 790. He defended the southern frontier against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, fighting at the Orbieu in 793 and taking part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801. In 804 he founded the monastery of Gellone, now Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert, where he died as a monk; he was later venerated as a saint and celebrated in the chansons de geste.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-of-gellone.jpg" alt="William of Gellone" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guercino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guercino_Guglielmo_d%27Aquitania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the mid-8th century to Thierry IV, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a>, and his wife Aldana. He was a relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, though the link is only speculated. It may have come through his mother, perhaps a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, or through Thierry, who was apparently a close kinsman of the maternal great-grandmother of Charlemagne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrada-of-prum">Bertrada of Prüm</a>. The two explanations are not mutually exclusive. As a kinsman and trusted companion, William spent his youth at the court of Charlemagne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 788 Chorso, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, was captured by the Basque Adalric and made to swear allegiance to Lupus II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. After his release <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> replaced him with his Frankish cousin William, in 790. William then subdued the Gascons. In 793 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba">Hisham I</a>, who had succeeded Abd ar-Rahman I, proclaimed a holy war against the Christians of the north and raised an army of 100,000 men.</p><p>Half attacked the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>; the other half invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>. William fought this force twice. He defeated it first, then met it again near the river Orbieu at Villedaigne and was beaten, but his obstinate resistance exhausted the invaders so badly that they withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. In 801 William commanded, with Louis, King of Aquitaine, a large expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, Provençals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitanians</a>, Gascons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> that captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 804 William founded the abbey of Gellone, now Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert, near Lodève, and placed it under the general control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-aniane">Benedict of Aniane</a>. Among his gifts was a piece of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>, which according to the Vita of William came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, who had received it from the Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He retired to Gellone as a monk in 806 and died there.</p><p>Pilgrims came in such numbers that his body was moved from the narthex to a place under the choir, to the displeasure of the Abbey of Aniane; forged documents on both sides leave the details doubtful. He was canonised in 1066 by Pope Alexander II. The Chanson de Guillaume of about 1140 opens a cycle of chansons de geste about him, where he is nicknamed Fièrebrace and the marquis au court nez, and legend gave him a Saracen wife, Orable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William of Gellone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a Frankish noble, Chorson, as count of Toulouse; after Chorson was captured by Basques and deposed in 790, the county passed to <b>William of Gellone</b>, a kinsman of the king, who defended the Spanish March against Umayyad raids and took part in the capture of Barcelona in 801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aquitaine, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.6,0&amp;year=792" aria-label="Aquitaine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William of Gellone"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5000 3765h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5000 3765h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.6,0&amp;year=792">What was here in 792 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
